1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,500
They're everywhere, in our homes, our cars,

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,140
our very bodies.

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:10,040
They sustain modern life and are essential to every advanced

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,640
tool built by human beings.

5
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:17,180
They can take us to the heavens or destroy us.

6
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:22,860
Now, heavy metals on Modern Marvel.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,380
They come from the dawn of the cosmos

8
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:45,380
and the fiery hearts of dying stars.

9
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:48,080
Heavy metals are cooked in the center of a star.

10
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:50,380
As a star is born and lives its life,

11
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:54,320
it comes to a phase at the end where a bunch of processes

12
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:56,760
happen very rapidly, and the star explodes.

13
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,000
And in exploding, scatters out a group of heavy metals

14
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:05,000
that reach all the corners of the universe.

15
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,480
Metals now found on Earth came from one of two sources,

16
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:13,480
space debris that collected as the planet formed

17
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:19,080
4.6 billion years ago, or meteorites

18
00:01:19,180 --> 00:01:23,060
that have since struck.

19
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,700
They endure through an eternal life

20
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:28,500
cycle of endless transformation.

21
00:01:28,501 --> 00:01:33,140
They are found, manipulated, relied upon in ways that we

22
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:36,180
totally take for granted, and then they're discarded and

23
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:37,580
reused again.

24
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:41,540
They're completely recyclable in most cases.

25
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:44,820
Metals make up 2 thirds of the known elements.

26
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:47,980
An element is defined by the number of protons, neutrons,

27
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:51,520
and electrons that form its atoms.

28
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:54,900
The periodic table lists elements in ascending scale

29
00:01:54,901 --> 00:01:57,600
based on atomic number, the number of protons

30
00:01:57,700 --> 00:01:59,840
in each nucleus.

31
00:01:59,940 --> 00:02:02,780
A periodic table is a way to organize the elements that

32
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,140
occur naturally in the universe and ones that

33
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,240
can be created by unnatural processes into a sequence that

34
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:11,680
starts with the lightest element, hydrogen,

35
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:14,480
and progresses up to the heaviest natural element,

36
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:15,580
uranium.

37
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:19,020
And on the progression, somewhere around 26, iron,

38
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:22,300
is a break point where anything heavier could be regarded as a

39
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,900
heavy metal.

40
00:02:24,901 --> 00:02:29,480
From the earliest days, human progress has been tied

41
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:32,100
to the mastery of metals.

42
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:35,180
The advanced civilizations all use metals

43
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:40,480
for cooking their food, for transportation, for work,

44
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,820
for agriculture, for defense.

45
00:02:45,320 --> 00:02:49,020
The average American baby born in 2006

46
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:53,060
will require 45,000 pounds of metals during its lifetime

47
00:02:53,061 --> 00:02:54,860
to sustain its standard of living.

48
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,100
Heavy metals such as iron, lead, zinc, nickel, and copper

49
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:05,840
are so essential to America's economic and military might

50
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:10,680
that they are stored in the national defense stockpile,

51
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:13,440
a $10 billion collection of materials

52
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:15,380
ready in case of all out war.

53
00:03:15,381 --> 00:03:16,380
war.

54
00:03:20,380 --> 00:03:24,180
In a national emergency, no metal would likely prove more

55
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:27,120
decisive than uranium.

56
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:30,960
With an atomic number of 92, uranium is the heaviest

57
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:35,860
of all elements, 18.7 times denser than water.

58
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,640
Its naturally occurring radiation generates tremendous

59
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:42,400
power from within the Earth's core, more than twice the level

60
00:03:42,401 --> 00:03:46,140
of global human energy consumption.

61
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:48,940
You can account for about 30 trillion watts

62
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:53,640
in the form of radiation coming from the uranium-238 that

63
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:57,140
was captured when the early Earth was formed in the core.

64
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:01,080
That now acts like a small nuclear reactor.

65
00:04:01,180 --> 00:04:03,020
It's a natural source of heat that

66
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,120
has to make its way to the outside of the planet.

67
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,060
And it brings up quite a bit.

68
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,520
And volcanoes today are living proof

69
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:11,600
that something in the core of the Earth

70
00:04:11,601 --> 00:04:13,100
is generating a lot of heat.

71
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:22,080
The introduction of the atomic bomb in 1945

72
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:24,140
heralded uranium's immense potential

73
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:27,280
as a destructive force.

74
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:30,060
But splitting the atom also unleashed a vast, peaceful

75
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:31,160
source of energy.

76
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:38,860
There are 103 nuclear reactors operating

77
00:04:38,861 --> 00:04:42,900
operating across the United States, generating nearly 20%

78
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,200
of the nation's electricity.

79
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:53,700
Worldwide, 430 reactors help power 31 different countries.

80
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,020
Like other metals, uranium is extracted through either

81
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:05,220
underground or open pit mining.

82
00:05:05,320 --> 00:05:08,020
The ore is pulverized, then treated with acid

83
00:05:08,021 --> 00:05:11,220
to dissolve out the uranium.

84
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:15,760
Uranium is radioactive, but it's not super radioactive.

85
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:17,660
You can hold ore in your hands.

86
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:20,760
You can actually come up to a fresh fuel assembly

87
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:23,260
and inspect it very closely.

88
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,140
Uranium must be heavily processed

89
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:27,680
to produce usable fuel.

90
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:31,540
It's converted into a gas so that uranium-235,

91
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:34,640
the fissile isotope needed to create a chain reaction,

92
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:40,880
can be enriched from its natural state of 1% to 3% to 5%.

93
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:44,820
After enrichment, the gas is reformed into uranium dioxide

94
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,760
and molded into fuel pellets.

95
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:50,460
The pellets are placed inside metal tubes

96
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:53,960
and assembled into bundles for the reactor core.

97
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:57,560
What I have in front of me is a simulated fuel bundle.

98
00:05:57,660 --> 00:06:01,400
This fuel bundle is made up of fuel rods and cooling water

99
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:02,340
rods.

100
00:06:02,341 --> 00:06:04,980
And these little rods that you see in front of me

101
00:06:04,981 --> 00:06:08,180
are what house the fuel pellets that are used

102
00:06:08,181 --> 00:06:10,140
to generate power in these bundles.

103
00:06:10,141 --> 00:06:12,780
The bottom is called a fuel support piece,

104
00:06:12,781 --> 00:06:15,480
which allows the bundle to rest in the core.

105
00:06:15,481 --> 00:06:18,580
The top here is what's called the bail handle,

106
00:06:18,581 --> 00:06:21,500
which is used to maneuver this fuel bundle around

107
00:06:21,501 --> 00:06:26,000
or to offload the bundle out of the core.

108
00:06:26,001 --> 00:06:28,000
The civilian nuclear power industry

109
00:06:28,001 --> 00:06:32,340
grew out of the Cold War and the global arms race.

110
00:06:32,341 --> 00:06:35,440
Ironically, fully half of the uranium that powers American

111
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:38,640
reactors now comes from dismantled Soviet nuclear

112
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:40,240
weapons.

113
00:06:40,340 --> 00:06:44,380
We get an economical source of the uranium,

114
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:48,320
and better yet, about 10,000 Russian warheads have been

115
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:50,720
destroyed in this process.

116
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:52,820
And under the deal with Russia right now,

117
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:58,300
it'll be 20,000 weapons destroyed and used in US

118
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:02,340
reactors to produce electricity here.

119
00:07:02,341 --> 00:07:05,740
Nuclear fission converts matter into energy that's released

120
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:08,820
as immense amounts of heat.

121
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:12,220
In nuclear reactors, uranium acts as a trigger,

122
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:14,320
the initial power source for what eventually

123
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:17,360
becomes electricity.

124
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:21,320
In a nuclear power plant, the uranium forms the heat source,

125
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:24,000
and that heat is then transferred over to water,

126
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:25,400
which heats the water.

127
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:28,640
And the hot water does the actual work of driving a turbine,

128
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:32,340
very much as it would in a coal-fired or an oil-fired plant.

129
00:07:32,341 --> 00:07:35,340
But nuclear fission is supremely potent.

130
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:42,340
A uranium pellet the size of a peanut generates the same amount

131
00:07:42,341 --> 00:07:48,340
of energy as 1,780 pounds of coal, or 149 gallons of oil.

132
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:54,340
Underneath that pool of water and that stainless steel head

133
00:07:54,341 --> 00:07:56,340
is a dry well compartment.

134
00:07:56,341 --> 00:07:59,340
That compartment contains the reactor vessel.

135
00:07:59,341 --> 00:08:02,540
That reactor is currently operating at 100% power,

136
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:07,280
supplying the energy needs of over 1 million people.

137
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:09,680
The most visible part of a nuclear power plant,

138
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:12,280
the cooling tower, is actually the safest.

139
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:18,120
Towers like this one at Grand Gulf Station in Fort Gibson,

140
00:08:18,220 --> 00:08:21,820
Mississippi merely release steam, which is a byproduct

141
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,160
of the generation process.

142
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:27,640
The vapor is inert, and a cooling tower could be destroyed

143
00:08:27,641 --> 00:08:29,880
could be destroyed without releasing any radiation

144
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:30,780
into the atmosphere.

145
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:40,080
The reactor is the heart of a nuclear plant.

146
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:43,080
Elaborate security systems, multiple engineering

147
00:08:43,180 --> 00:08:45,800
redundancies, and backup power sources

148
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:48,800
all have a single ultimate goal, to ensure

149
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:51,900
that the nuclear rods stay underwater and under control.

150
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:57,140
The concern is making sure we always have enough water.

151
00:08:57,141 --> 00:08:59,680
to keep a sufficient level above top of active fuel

152
00:08:59,780 --> 00:09:04,680
to keep from having the fuel bundles get too hot.

153
00:09:04,780 --> 00:09:07,120
The bundles generate such intense heat

154
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:09,460
that if not adequately cooled by water,

155
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:12,060
they would quickly begin to melt their containment vessel

156
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:14,860
and release radioactivity.

157
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:16,860
This happened at Chernobyl in 1986.

158
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:24,500
Nuclear rods decay over time, and after five years

159
00:09:24,501 --> 00:09:28,600
from the reactor to a nearby spent fuel pool.

160
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:31,980
Uranium isotopes continue to radiate considerable heat,

161
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:35,140
so rods cool in the pool for 10 to 20 years

162
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,540
before they're ready for dry storage.

163
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:41,880
Because they remain radioactive for thousands of years,

164
00:09:41,980 --> 00:09:45,380
safe long-term storage presents a difficult and as yet

165
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:46,920
unresolved dilemma.

166
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,620
While nuclear power and atomic weaponry

167
00:09:52,621 --> 00:09:56,420
the same raw material, uranium-235.

168
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:59,860
A warhead requires uranium at a bomb-grade purity

169
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:02,000
of nearly 90%.

170
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,300
This mountain range north of Moab, Utah,

171
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,300
played a unique role in the history of uranium mining.

172
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:18,920
When the Soviet Union shocked the West by detonating its first

173
00:10:18,921 --> 00:10:23,660
its first atomic bomb in 1949, Cold War fears raged.

174
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:28,900
In Washington, the Atomic Energy Commission

175
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,060
decided to enlist civilian help to build the nation's

176
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:32,860
uranium reserve.

177
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:38,800
The AEC raised the base price for uranium ore

178
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:44,180
and offered a $10,000 bonus for the highest grade material.

179
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:47,540
Eastern Utah was well known for its uranium deposits

180
00:10:47,541 --> 00:10:50,880
and thousands flocked to the region.

181
00:10:50,980 --> 00:10:53,240
Anyone with guts, vision, and luck

182
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:56,980
had a chance to make their fortune.

183
00:10:57,080 --> 00:10:59,860
But few possessed those qualities in greater abundance

184
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:04,760
than an unemployed Texas geologist named Charlie Steen.

185
00:11:04,860 --> 00:11:08,220
Charlie hitchhiked down Highway 191

186
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:12,060
and walked in here for three days at a time.

187
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:14,100
He'd pack all of his food and water.

188
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:16,640
And then he wandered all through the hills and the canyons

189
00:11:16,641 --> 00:11:21,640
around here looking for his idea of what would be favorable

190
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:25,640
areas for the discovery of uranium.

191
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:29,120
In 1952, Charlie drilled a discovery hole

192
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:31,760
in the Mavita Plateau.

193
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:35,320
And he had to borrow a friend's Geiger counter

194
00:11:35,420 --> 00:11:39,660
to actually discover that, yes, it was uranium ore.

195
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,460
Charlie Steen had discovered the single largest deposit

196
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,800
of uranium in the United States.

197
00:11:45,801 --> 00:11:50,300
Soon, this tunnel was built to exploit his claim.

198
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:54,200
Word of the find triggered a human stampede.

199
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:56,640
There were all kinds of magazine articles about,

200
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:59,240
come down and get rich, you know, buy this Geiger counter.

201
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:01,240
It's guaranteed to find you something.

202
00:12:01,340 --> 00:12:02,460
It was just like the gold rush.

203
00:12:02,560 --> 00:12:08,160
There was a lot of hype and misinformation.

204
00:12:08,260 --> 00:12:11,760
There's legends, you know, stories of people renting

205
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:15,260
small planes and throwing claim stakes out of the planes.

206
00:12:15,261 --> 00:12:18,160
to try to cover as much ground as they could

207
00:12:18,260 --> 00:12:20,260
and tie up as much as they could.

208
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:23,260
The Salt Lake Stock Exchange went wild.

209
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:29,000
It was selling penny-ante stock in order to develop the mines

210
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:31,000
to get them going.

211
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:35,720
Virtually overnight, Moab went from a sleepy hamlet of 1,200

212
00:12:35,820 --> 00:12:38,260
to a boomtown of 5,000.

213
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:40,760
All dreamed of striking it rich.

214
00:12:40,860 --> 00:12:43,760
Few actually did.

215
00:12:43,761 --> 00:12:46,460
I was like some others, just going along

216
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:50,400
and checking every rock that might look like I have an ore

217
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:51,400
in it.

218
00:12:51,500 --> 00:12:54,700
Sometimes you'd get excited, and sometimes you wouldn't,

219
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:57,100
because there's a lot of different rocks

220
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:00,000
that do have radiation.

221
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:02,820
The AEC eventually phased out subsidies

222
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,520
to private miners.

223
00:13:04,620 --> 00:13:07,420
The uranium market stabilized, and like all rushes,

224
00:13:07,520 --> 00:13:11,620
the great Utah boom turned to bust.

225
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:15,620
But Moab would never be the same.

226
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:18,700
MOAB WAS THE FIRST PLACE IN UTAH THAT HAD LIQUOR BY THE

227
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:19,600
DRINK.

228
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:20,900
IT CHANGED THE CULTURE.

229
00:13:20,960 --> 00:13:22,200
IT CHANGED THE ECONOMY.

230
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:24,740
IT MADE A MUCH MORE DIVERSE CULTURE HERE.

231
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:28,640
IT HAD A HUGE IMPACT ON MOAB.

232
00:13:28,700 --> 00:13:31,700
TODAY, RISING OIL PRICES HAVE RENEWED INTEREST IN

233
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:34,340
PEACEFUL NUCLEAR POWER.

234
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:37,120
THE UNITED STATES IS ON THE VERGE OF GRANTING PERMITS FOR

235
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:40,340
THE FIRST NEW CONSTRUCTION OF NUCLEAR POWER PLANTS IN 30

236
00:13:40,341 --> 00:13:42,740
years.

237
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:46,240
And miners are once again scouting the hills near Moab,

238
00:13:46,340 --> 00:13:48,940
betting that uranium will one day make them rich.

239
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:56,080
Uranium plays a key role in 21st century life.

240
00:13:56,180 --> 00:13:59,000
The vast construction projects that helped define the 20th

241
00:13:59,100 --> 00:14:03,000
century were made possible by the heavy metal iron and its

242
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:04,000
steel alloys.

243
00:14:07,700 --> 00:14:09,540
The accident at the Chernobyl reactor

244
00:14:09,541 --> 00:14:13,880
released about 400 times as much radiation as the atomic bomb

245
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:15,220
dropped on Hiroshima.

246
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:30,020
We live in a world of steel, which

247
00:14:30,100 --> 00:14:33,400
has the combination of strength and flexibility that makes

248
00:14:33,460 --> 00:14:35,900
ideal frames for large modern structures.

249
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:42,780
Steel, which is stronger and more pliant than iron,

250
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:46,840
is simply iron plus less than 1% carbon.

251
00:14:46,940 --> 00:14:49,480
Iron has an atomic number of 26.

252
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:56,060
Today, metal production is dominated

253
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:59,960
by recently devised steel alloys,

254
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:02,400
steels mixed with a variety of heavy metals.

255
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,940
Today, approximately 20% of the steel

256
00:15:07,941 --> 00:15:11,180
that goes into a typical car wasn't even produced 10 years

257
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:12,040
ago.

258
00:15:12,140 --> 00:15:15,820
By 2010, that number is expected to increase to probably about

259
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:19,680
50%, which shows how rapidly the materials are changing in

260
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:20,580
automobiles.

261
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:30,600
These fiery furnaces at Middle Steel USA, outside Chicago,

262
00:15:30,700 --> 00:15:33,600
appear much as steel factories did a century ago.

263
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:41,340
And the basic stages of production, smelting, melting,

264
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:43,840
and casting, are also much the same.

265
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:48,520
But here, up to a dozen other metals

266
00:15:48,620 --> 00:15:51,620
are added to the molten steel in precise quantities

267
00:15:51,720 --> 00:15:54,560
in a closely guarded recipe.

268
00:15:54,660 --> 00:15:58,260
Even minute amounts of selected minerals, sometimes as low as

269
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:01,900
one part in 5,000, can change the properties of the final

270
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:02,800
product.

271
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,400
High-strength alloy steels form the safety

272
00:16:07,401 --> 00:16:12,400
carriages of cars. Special armor alloys gird

273
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:16,400
naval vessels, protecting against corrosion from seawater,

274
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:23,120
and the impact of torpedoes. These so-called super alloys

275
00:16:23,220 --> 00:16:25,260
have made steel more valuable than ever.

276
00:16:28,420 --> 00:16:31,860
But before our modern age of steel, other heavy metals

277
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:35,800
had their day. Today's periodic table lists

278
00:16:35,801 --> 00:16:37,600
86 metals.

279
00:16:37,700 --> 00:16:42,100
But until the 12th century AD, humans had discovered just seven,

280
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:45,940
all heavy, known as the metals of antiquity.

281
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:50,440
Gold and silver were soft and used mostly for ornamentation.

282
00:16:50,540 --> 00:16:52,880
Mercury, also known as quicksilver,

283
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:56,020
is the only metal that is liquid at room temperature.

284
00:16:56,120 --> 00:16:58,520
It was widely used to dissolve gold and silver.

285
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:05,060
Tin, lead, and iron served important functions,

286
00:17:05,061 --> 00:17:08,260
but only after the age of the seventh metal of antiquity,

287
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:10,500
copper.

288
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:12,860
Some of the oldest copper relics

289
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:15,540
are found in a cave outside of Israel

290
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:20,100
where it's very ambiguous sculptural forms and fashion

291
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:21,880
very skillfully in copper.

292
00:17:21,980 --> 00:17:25,880
So there is some sort of circumstantial evidence

293
00:17:25,980 --> 00:17:28,420
that states that the fascination for the material

294
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:31,460
and aesthetics and sensibility were the forerunners

295
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:35,060
to the functional utilization of the material.

296
00:17:35,061 --> 00:17:39,540
Approximately 10,000 years ago, copper became the first metal

297
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:42,300
manipulated by man.

298
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:46,800
But copper tools were rare until the discovery of smelting, the

299
00:17:46,900 --> 00:17:50,380
process of heating copper oxide to produce greater and purer

300
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:51,680
quantities of copper.

301
00:17:53,620 --> 00:17:57,580
So the copper age actually started to begin around 4500

302
00:17:57,680 --> 00:17:59,920
BC when they were actually smelting the copper as opposed

303
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:02,080
to finding found metallic copper.

304
00:18:02,081 --> 00:18:04,480
copper.

305
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:06,880
Copper could be formed, hammered, and sharpened

306
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:09,320
into tools stronger than the stone and bone implements

307
00:18:09,420 --> 00:18:11,320
previously used by early man.

308
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:15,820
Copper tools and weapons were soon improved

309
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:18,320
by the addition of other metals.

310
00:18:18,420 --> 00:18:21,740
The next stage in development was to combine metals.

311
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:25,740
So for example, combining copper and tin gave you bronze.

312
00:18:25,840 --> 00:18:28,040
It's harder than either copper or tin,

313
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:30,780
so it made superior weapons.

314
00:18:30,781 --> 00:18:35,380
Better weapons meant the capacity to conquer and rule.

315
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:39,820
By 2000 BC, metals were essential commodities,

316
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:44,100
powerful forces shaping the destinies of empires.

317
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:47,160
The early cities were basically built around trade

318
00:18:47,260 --> 00:18:48,840
in many of these objects.

319
00:18:48,940 --> 00:18:52,440
And one finds tin coming from Anatolia and Turkey.

320
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:54,840
One finds copper coming from Africa.

321
00:18:54,940 --> 00:18:57,340
Trade in metals was an enormous part of the economy.

322
00:19:00,780 --> 00:19:04,580
Metals possessed such miraculous properties that many ancients

323
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:07,660
believed they were alive.

324
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:11,520
In the third century BC, the philosopher Aristotle theorized

325
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:15,660
that metals grew underground, much like seeds.

326
00:19:15,700 --> 00:19:19,140
The ancients appreciated iron's special properties of strength

327
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:20,440
and malleability.

328
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,740
But before iron could be shaped, it had to be heated to near its

329
00:19:26,741 --> 00:19:31,980
to near its melting temperature of 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit.

330
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:33,440
Such heat was difficult to produce

331
00:19:33,540 --> 00:19:37,380
until roughly 1,000 BC, when a new technique raised furnace

332
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:38,480
temperatures dramatically.

333
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,100
The lever's attached to a bellows.

334
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:48,000
A bellows is an air pump.

335
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:51,140
By blowing air into the fire, we can just about double

336
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:51,940
the temperature.

337
00:19:56,740 --> 00:20:00,640
When I put the bar in the fire, it's at a black heat,

338
00:20:00,740 --> 00:20:02,740
below 1,000 degrees.

339
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:06,180
As it heats up, it'll turn red at about 1,000 degrees,

340
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:10,780
then go from red to orange, to yellow, to white hot.

341
00:20:10,880 --> 00:20:14,020
White hot is close to the melting temperature,

342
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:16,700
about 2,700 degrees Fahrenheit.

343
00:20:21,700 --> 00:20:24,800
Iron is abundant and thus inexpensive.

344
00:20:24,801 --> 00:20:28,800
It's strong, durable, and can be forged and welded into pieces

345
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:30,800
of virtually any shape and size.

346
00:20:34,300 --> 00:20:37,740
Since well before Christ, iron reigned as the best substance

347
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:40,820
for making everything from household goods to weapons of

348
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:42,820
war.

349
00:20:42,920 --> 00:20:46,560
In the 18th century, iron masters changed their fuel from

350
00:20:46,660 --> 00:20:50,500
charcoal to coke, a charcoal derivative that burns harder

351
00:20:50,600 --> 00:20:54,800
and hotter, and constructed much larger furnaces.

352
00:20:54,801 --> 00:20:58,800
The massive iron foundry in Colebrookdale, England,

353
00:20:58,900 --> 00:21:02,800
burned 24-7, filling the night sky with a crimson glow

354
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:07,800
that one observer compared to a descent into hell itself.

355
00:21:07,900 --> 00:21:10,800
Colebrookdale, I think, like a lot of other fire-powered

356
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:13,800
industries, must have been a pretty amazing thing for people

357
00:21:13,900 --> 00:21:14,800
to see.

358
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:17,800
The night sky is lit up with flames and the sky black with

359
00:21:17,900 --> 00:21:18,800
smoke.

360
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:22,800
And while today we kind of look at that as being an incarnation

361
00:21:22,801 --> 00:21:25,200
of some of the bad things about industry.

362
00:21:25,300 --> 00:21:28,000
In the 18th and 19th century, it was really seen as signs

363
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:32,740
of progress and something that people were really proud about.

364
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:35,340
In the 19th century, steel replaced iron

365
00:21:35,440 --> 00:21:37,240
as our primary construction material.

366
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:42,760
Blacksmiths had long struggled to remove the existing carbon

367
00:21:42,860 --> 00:21:46,060
from iron and then add back precisely the right amount

368
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:47,260
to create steel.

369
00:21:47,261 --> 00:21:54,400
In 1856, English engineer Henry Bessemer discovered that blowing

370
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:58,000
air through liquid cast iron created a reaction that

371
00:21:58,100 --> 00:22:01,580
generated heat and removed carbon at the same time.

372
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:04,540
By varying the airflow, the carbon content could be

373
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:05,640
controlled.

374
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:12,920
Iron and steel created the Industrial Revolution.

375
00:22:12,921 --> 00:22:17,920
They formed the tracks of the Transcontinental Railroad

376
00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:20,260
and the trains that roared along them.

377
00:22:22,360 --> 00:22:25,060
And they made possible the skyscrapers that came to

378
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:30,440
symbolize the sublime reach of 20th century civilization.

379
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:33,180
If you imagine a structure to take a wind load,

380
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:34,680
the wind is going to tilt it.

381
00:22:34,780 --> 00:22:37,720
And as soon as you tilt it, you bow the outside edge here,

382
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:40,320
and now you're pulling on this curved side,

383
00:22:40,321 --> 00:22:43,120
side, and you're pushing on this curved side.

384
00:22:43,220 --> 00:22:46,120
And so that material has to be not only strong

385
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:48,720
on the compressive side, it has to be strong on this side too,

386
00:22:48,820 --> 00:22:50,120
and stone won't do.

387
00:22:50,220 --> 00:22:51,220
Steel will.

388
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:54,060
A steel building can rock in the wind quite a long way

389
00:22:54,160 --> 00:22:57,080
and still survive.

390
00:22:57,180 --> 00:23:00,780
Steel cables act much like rope, at once flexible

391
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:02,480
and strong.

392
00:23:02,580 --> 00:23:05,080
The Brooklyn Bridge was the first large-scale structure

393
00:23:05,180 --> 00:23:07,320
supported by steel cables.

394
00:23:07,321 --> 00:23:11,560
And its sweeping span contains an astonishing 14,000 miles

395
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:14,460
of steel wire.

396
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:17,160
And the workers, once the caissons were built

397
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:19,800
and the pylons and everything came up,

398
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:24,080
they stood atop the marvel of all history in terms of iron

399
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:28,580
working and looked down in a way that man had never seen before.

400
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:30,480
Those men atop the Brooklyn Bridge

401
00:23:30,580 --> 00:23:33,780
looked out on the emerging Manhattan skyline,

402
00:23:33,781 --> 00:23:37,180
A vista that would soon embody a century built by steel.

403
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:43,620
But workers who toiled with other heavy metals, like lead,

404
00:23:43,720 --> 00:23:46,720
would soon learn that progress has its price.

405
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:55,940
More steel in the United States is used to make bottle caps

406
00:23:56,040 --> 00:23:58,240
than to manufacture automobile bodies.

407
00:23:58,241 --> 00:23:59,140
bodies.

408
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:07,280
With an atomic number of 82, lead is extremely dense,

409
00:24:07,281 --> 00:24:10,660
the best natural shield known to man.

410
00:24:10,661 --> 00:24:13,020
Cathode ray tubes from television and computer

411
00:24:13,021 --> 00:24:17,800
screens contain enough lead to block harmful radiation.

412
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:21,260
Medical x-rays use lead for the same reason.

413
00:24:21,261 --> 00:24:23,560
And a thick enough lead container can even safely

414
00:24:23,561 --> 00:24:26,560
store nuclear waste.

415
00:24:26,561 --> 00:24:28,660
As a particle tries to pass through it,

416
00:24:28,760 --> 00:24:30,700
it has to collide with a lot of atoms,

417
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,400
each of which has a lot of protons, these charged particles,

418
00:24:34,500 --> 00:24:36,900
that slow or stop the process.

419
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,300
And so it's an extremely good shielding material.

420
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:43,720
Today, the most common application of lead

421
00:24:43,820 --> 00:24:47,020
is in car batteries.

422
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:49,320
About 80% of the lead that's produced in this country

423
00:24:49,420 --> 00:24:50,820
goes into lead acid batteries.

424
00:24:50,920 --> 00:24:53,860
It's still the cheapest form of electrical power

425
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:55,120
for the automobile.

426
00:24:55,121 --> 00:24:57,760
We have over 200 million automobiles in this country.

427
00:24:57,860 --> 00:25:00,760
Every one of them has at least one battery.

428
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:03,120
One of the world's largest deposits of lead ore

429
00:25:03,220 --> 00:25:05,120
lies beneath the St. Francis Mountains

430
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:07,860
in southeast Missouri.

431
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:11,000
Millions of years ago, this region was an ocean,

432
00:25:11,100 --> 00:25:12,980
and deposits formed in a narrow vein

433
00:25:13,080 --> 00:25:17,200
along an ancient tropical reef, a vein 42 miles long

434
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:20,340
and just 500 feet wide.

435
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:22,840
Eventually, shifts in the Earth's tectonic plates

436
00:25:22,841 --> 00:25:26,140
covered the sea and the lead vein with a mountain range

437
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:27,340
that exists today.

438
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:36,880
The middle lies 1,000 feet beneath the surface,

439
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:39,180
and every man and machine used to mine it

440
00:25:39,280 --> 00:25:41,200
must descend through this elevator shaft.

441
00:25:41,201 --> 00:25:42,700
shaft.

442
00:25:51,340 --> 00:25:54,580
Large haul trucks, which can measure 40 feet long and 15

443
00:25:54,680 --> 00:25:57,780
feet wide, are dismantled for transport

444
00:25:57,880 --> 00:25:59,860
and reassembled underground.

445
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:03,820
These machines will never again see sunlight.

446
00:26:03,920 --> 00:26:06,920
We have to maintain all of our equipment underground.

447
00:26:07,020 --> 00:26:10,060
It's not like we can drive it to the nearest service station.

448
00:26:10,061 --> 00:26:12,560
We need to change our large tires underground.

449
00:26:12,660 --> 00:26:14,300
We even rebuild engines.

450
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:16,860
But all the maintenance needs have to take place underground

451
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:19,200
because we only have shaft access to the mines.

452
00:26:23,900 --> 00:26:27,720
Lead ore's exceptional density supports tunnels 30 feet wide

453
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:29,620
and up to 120 feet high.

454
00:26:36,820 --> 00:26:40,060
Hydraulic jumbo diggers with two front-loaded drills

455
00:26:40,061 --> 00:26:43,000
out the first stage in lead mining.

456
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:45,300
We'll go in and we'll drill the rock face,

457
00:26:45,400 --> 00:26:46,400
put a series of holes.

458
00:26:46,500 --> 00:26:50,980
We'll actually use 75 holes in a rock face that are two inches

459
00:26:51,080 --> 00:26:55,280
in diameter and drill to a depth of 12 feet.

460
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:58,040
They're done on a specific engineered pattern

461
00:26:58,140 --> 00:27:02,220
to allow for the maximum breakage of that ore material.

462
00:27:02,320 --> 00:27:04,160
Most of the holes are drilled in the center

463
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:05,820
of the rock face.

464
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:08,160
That area will be detonated first,

465
00:27:08,161 --> 00:27:11,300
creating a void for other rock to break towards.

466
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:14,200
After drilling, those holes would then be loaded with

467
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:17,340
explosives in what we call the charging process.

468
00:27:17,440 --> 00:27:21,340
We'll use ammonium nitrate fuel oil, which is a blasting agent.

469
00:27:21,440 --> 00:27:24,920
We'll use dynamite, and we'll use blasting caps.

470
00:27:25,020 --> 00:27:27,840
This single ore base is filled with 800 pounds

471
00:27:27,940 --> 00:27:29,540
of explosives.

472
00:27:29,640 --> 00:27:33,680
The mine uses 19 different types of detonating caps, each with a

473
00:27:33,780 --> 00:27:37,180
different timing delay.

474
00:27:37,181 --> 00:27:39,180
The farther it is from the center,

475
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:41,180
the later an explosion takes place,

476
00:27:41,280 --> 00:27:43,680
maximizing the cascade effect.

477
00:27:43,780 --> 00:27:47,920
When the round's shot off, this here is instant, OK?

478
00:27:48,020 --> 00:27:52,220
It's followed by this next cap, which is a 1, OK?

479
00:27:52,320 --> 00:27:54,640
It's 500 milliseconds, OK?

480
00:27:54,740 --> 00:27:56,340
And it keeps going up by there.

481
00:27:56,341 --> 00:28:08,520
Once the rock is blasted free, giant mechanical scalers

482
00:28:08,620 --> 00:28:11,180
remove loose material from the walls and ceiling.

483
00:28:14,620 --> 00:28:18,000
Each day, 5,000 tons of rock are hauled to the surface.

484
00:28:20,740 --> 00:28:24,100
But less than 5% is lead, and a series of steps

485
00:28:24,101 --> 00:28:25,600
is required to liberate the metal.

486
00:28:29,100 --> 00:28:32,400
First, ore passes through a grinding circuit that crushes

487
00:28:32,500 --> 00:28:34,100
it into ever smaller pieces.

488
00:28:39,040 --> 00:28:42,260
Different minerals are then separated out in a process

489
00:28:42,360 --> 00:28:45,260
known as flotation.

490
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:48,460
Just as laundry detergent latches onto particles of dirt

491
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:51,600
and lifts them away from clothes, a flotation cell

492
00:28:51,601 --> 00:28:56,840
a flotation cell forms a chemical bond with its target metal.

493
00:28:56,940 --> 00:29:00,640
The flotation process is both a chemical and a physical process.

494
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:03,740
The collector latches onto the surface of the mineral particle,

495
00:29:03,840 --> 00:29:05,240
and as the air bubble comes along,

496
00:29:05,340 --> 00:29:07,680
it becomes more of a physical process at that time.

497
00:29:07,780 --> 00:29:10,780
A chemical bond attaches a collector to the mineral particle,

498
00:29:10,880 --> 00:29:13,660
and a physical reaction between the air and the mineral particle

499
00:29:13,760 --> 00:29:17,620
actually brings it to the surface.

500
00:29:17,720 --> 00:29:21,100
Most of the lead we use today was mined years ago

501
00:29:21,101 --> 00:29:22,600
been recycled.

502
00:29:22,700 --> 00:29:27,400
Over 98% of all lead is recycled in this country today.

503
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:30,340
What we're doing is we're recycling that lead that has

504
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:34,440
been produced in the past right back around for reuse.

505
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:37,480
The majority of recycled lead comes from car batteries.

506
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:42,680
Massive landfills once overflowed with the toxic

507
00:29:42,780 --> 00:29:44,220
remains of millions of them.

508
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,560
Today, batteries find new life at bunkers

509
00:29:49,561 --> 00:29:53,460
of holding more than 15,000 tons of shredded batteries.

510
00:29:56,500 --> 00:30:00,500
Recycled or mined, lead can be as harmful as it is helpful,

511
00:30:00,600 --> 00:30:04,900
a sobering troop dating back to antiquity.

512
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,620
Ancient Romans used it in dishes, coins, and vast systems

513
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:10,120
of water distribution.

514
00:30:12,220 --> 00:30:15,020
They even flavored food and drinks with the metal,

515
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:17,960
savoring its sweet overtones.

516
00:30:17,961 --> 00:30:20,460
Well, the ancient Romans were, in some ways,

517
00:30:20,560 --> 00:30:22,060
quite addicted to lead.

518
00:30:22,160 --> 00:30:25,400
There is a theory about lead playing this part

519
00:30:25,500 --> 00:30:27,200
in the fall of the Roman Empire.

520
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:30,600
There are accounts of Julius Caesar not being able to father

521
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:32,400
a child except for one.

522
00:30:32,500 --> 00:30:35,020
And his offspring was indifferent to sex

523
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:36,840
and couldn't father a child.

524
00:30:36,940 --> 00:30:41,620
So lead could have been a huge component in that.

525
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:44,520
In the modern era, lead's devastating impact

526
00:30:44,620 --> 00:30:46,860
is all too familiar.

527
00:30:46,861 --> 00:30:50,860
Beginning in the 1920s, refiners began adding it to gasoline

528
00:30:50,960 --> 00:30:53,360
to reduce engine knocking.

529
00:30:53,460 --> 00:30:56,500
The problem with it is that it has a high vapor pressure.

530
00:30:56,600 --> 00:31:00,500
It evaporates easily, and it can get away as you manufacture

531
00:31:00,600 --> 00:31:03,100
it and put it in the gasoline.

532
00:31:03,200 --> 00:31:06,520
Workers who made the additive learned firsthand how lead

533
00:31:06,620 --> 00:31:09,020
attacks and debilitates the central nervous system.

534
00:31:11,160 --> 00:31:14,920
It was termed as loony metal because in the latter stages

535
00:31:14,921 --> 00:31:16,920
of the involvement of the metal in their systems,

536
00:31:17,020 --> 00:31:18,920
they started to kind of lose their minds

537
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:20,420
and then eventually died.

538
00:31:23,420 --> 00:31:28,160
Leaded gasoline would endure into the 1980s.

539
00:31:28,260 --> 00:31:32,280
In 1995, the Environmental Protection Agency

540
00:31:32,380 --> 00:31:35,500
concluded that 5,000 Americans died annually

541
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:37,580
from different kinds of lead poisoning.

542
00:31:40,180 --> 00:31:42,840
Today, lead has been banned from gas, paint,

543
00:31:42,841 --> 00:31:44,540
paint and most household items.

544
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:53,480
Lead workers undergo blood tests every six months.

545
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:55,880
We're finally learning that lead's value

546
00:31:55,980 --> 00:31:59,520
must always be measured by how safely we mine and use it.

547
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,000
While lead's history is measured in millennia,

548
00:32:07,100 --> 00:32:11,660
the heavy metal zinc is a recent and increasingly important part

549
00:32:11,661 --> 00:32:13,360
of our grandest structures.

550
00:32:18,360 --> 00:32:21,400
In the 1450s, Johannes Gutenberg used lead

551
00:32:21,500 --> 00:32:23,900
to create the first metal movable type,

552
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:26,400
giving the masses access to books.

553
00:32:26,500 --> 00:32:30,420
He is reported to have stated, give me 26 soldiers of lead,

554
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:32,220
and I will conquer the world.

555
00:32:32,221 --> 00:32:33,120
world.

556
00:32:41,060 --> 00:32:44,260
Zinc is placed at 30 on the periodic table,

557
00:32:44,360 --> 00:32:48,300
and perhaps no heavy metal has more applications.

558
00:32:48,400 --> 00:32:51,680
It fights infections and helps heal surface wounds on the body.

559
00:32:54,480 --> 00:32:56,720
Its heat and ultraviolet reflecting properties

560
00:32:56,820 --> 00:33:00,980
make it an essential ingredient in sunblock.

561
00:33:00,981 --> 00:33:05,820
and on the space shuttle's heat-resistant tiles.

562
00:33:05,821 --> 00:33:08,620
And it fuels commerce by making die castings

563
00:33:08,621 --> 00:33:11,280
for the automotive, electrical, and hardware industries.

564
00:33:17,700 --> 00:33:22,760
But zinc's primary role is in corrosion resistance.

565
00:33:22,761 --> 00:33:28,260
It extends the life of our modern steel-based infrastructure.

566
00:33:28,261 --> 00:33:33,600
When coated with zinc, cars, ships, buildings and bridges,

567
00:33:33,601 --> 00:33:37,600
any steel structure will survive for years or decades longer.

568
00:33:41,500 --> 00:33:45,620
Zinc is used to galvanize steel, which puts a layer of zinc

569
00:33:45,621 --> 00:33:46,920
on top of the steel.

570
00:33:46,921 --> 00:33:50,320
And then when the oxygen or water in the air

571
00:33:50,321 --> 00:33:54,320
begins to attack the zinc, it quickly forms an oxide skin

572
00:33:54,321 --> 00:33:56,020
that prevents further attack.

573
00:33:56,021 --> 00:34:01,600
So it's a wonderful material to inhibit corrosion.

574
00:34:01,700 --> 00:34:04,400
Once mined and milled, zinc ore proceeds

575
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:07,400
to the smelting furnace.

576
00:34:07,500 --> 00:34:10,740
There, carbon monoxide reacts chemically with the ore

577
00:34:10,840 --> 00:34:13,340
to form a zinc vapor.

578
00:34:13,440 --> 00:34:15,840
That gas is then condensed.

579
00:34:15,940 --> 00:34:19,040
Finally, it's reheated into molten zinc

580
00:34:19,140 --> 00:34:22,780
and ready to be cast into everything from 55-pound slabs

581
00:34:22,880 --> 00:34:26,020
to 2,400-pound bingots.

582
00:34:26,021 --> 00:34:29,220
The operator now is using skimming boards to remove dross

583
00:34:29,320 --> 00:34:30,860
from the surface of the ingots.

584
00:34:30,960 --> 00:34:34,460
The dross forms because the zinc has been exposed to air

585
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:38,260
and forms a frothy material that ruins the zinc surface.

586
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:40,560
We want the product to go out with a nice smooth surface,

587
00:34:40,660 --> 00:34:42,980
so he skims those off with skimming boards

588
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:46,040
and then closes the top of the ingot mold

589
00:34:46,140 --> 00:34:48,080
and allows it to slowly cool and solidify.

590
00:34:51,280 --> 00:34:53,980
Larger ingots are shipped to galvanizing companies

591
00:34:53,981 --> 00:34:58,780
to be alloyed with steel. But before steel is galvanized,

592
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:03,360
it must be thoroughly cleaned. We have a sulfuric acid bath

593
00:35:03,460 --> 00:35:08,760
here. It's about 160 degrees, about 10% to 12% sulfuric acid.

594
00:35:08,860 --> 00:35:12,240
We use this to remove any rust that might be on the steel.

595
00:35:12,340 --> 00:35:14,800
We're trying to get the steel as clean as possible before the

596
00:35:14,900 --> 00:35:20,400
galvanizing process. Here at Young Galvanizing

597
00:35:20,401 --> 00:35:21,660
near Pittsburgh.

598
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:26,040
The galvanizing kettle is 48 feet long, 5 feet wide,

599
00:35:26,140 --> 00:35:29,980
and 8 feet deep, big enough to submerge the largest pieces

600
00:35:30,080 --> 00:35:32,380
of steel.

601
00:35:32,480 --> 00:35:34,880
The steel is immersed in zinc until it reaches a bath

602
00:35:34,980 --> 00:35:37,380
temperature of 840 degrees Fahrenheit.

603
00:35:41,020 --> 00:35:44,860
The zinc and steel actually form a metallurgical bond, an alloy

604
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:47,520
that will resist rust and corrosion for many years to come.

605
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:53,240
Zinc has thus fulfilled its role as the great protector.

606
00:35:57,240 --> 00:35:59,440
Just as zinc galvanizing supports our visible

607
00:35:59,540 --> 00:36:02,840
infrastructure, copper forms the hidden foundation

608
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:03,940
of modern life.

609
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:12,260
Our world is inconceivable without electricity,

610
00:36:12,360 --> 00:36:14,260
and every inch of every electrical system

611
00:36:14,360 --> 00:36:16,660
depends on the unique conductive properties

612
00:36:16,760 --> 00:36:17,760
of copper wiring.

613
00:36:20,400 --> 00:36:22,800
Copper in today's world is intricately woven

614
00:36:22,900 --> 00:36:25,040
into the fabric of our society.

615
00:36:25,140 --> 00:36:27,540
The fact that roughly 400 pounds of it

616
00:36:27,640 --> 00:36:30,940
exist in the everyday home and that people use it unknowingly

617
00:36:31,040 --> 00:36:33,640
in electronics and telephone communication

618
00:36:33,740 --> 00:36:37,520
has permeated our culture in a way that has never been seen

619
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:38,320
before in history.

620
00:36:41,780 --> 00:36:44,360
For the past century, the world's largest source

621
00:36:44,460 --> 00:36:47,660
of raw copper has been a gargantuan open pit mine

622
00:36:47,760 --> 00:36:48,460
in Utah.

623
00:36:50,400 --> 00:36:53,100
You're looking at the largest man-made excavation on Earth,

624
00:36:53,200 --> 00:36:55,340
the Bingham Canyon Mine.

625
00:36:55,440 --> 00:36:57,500
From the point that I stand here,

626
00:36:57,600 --> 00:37:00,940
it's almost 2 1⁄2 miles to the other side.

627
00:37:01,040 --> 00:37:06,180
And in depth, it's over 3 quarters of a mile deep.

628
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:08,160
You could stack three Empire State buildings

629
00:37:08,260 --> 00:37:11,360
on top of each other, and they wouldn't reach the top.

630
00:37:14,700 --> 00:37:17,500
The Bingham Canyon Mine is one of the very few man-made

631
00:37:17,501 --> 00:37:19,000
structures visible from space.

632
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:26,840
Bingham has yielded more than $6 billion in copper,

633
00:37:26,940 --> 00:37:29,840
more than the California and Klondike gold rushes

634
00:37:29,940 --> 00:37:31,940
and Nevada's Comstock load combined.

635
00:37:35,760 --> 00:37:38,860
Each day, 450,000 tons of material

636
00:37:38,960 --> 00:37:40,960
are removed from the pit.

637
00:37:41,060 --> 00:37:44,060
But Bingham has been so heavily mined that today, its ore

638
00:37:44,160 --> 00:37:47,500
contains just 0.6% copper.

639
00:37:47,501 --> 00:37:54,500
After the rock is blasted free and crushed,

640
00:37:54,600 --> 00:37:57,980
a series of conveyor belts carries it five miles

641
00:37:58,080 --> 00:38:00,140
to the concentrator.

642
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:06,380
There, flotation cells separate out a liquid that's 28% copper.

643
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:10,860
The liquid is pumped 17 miles to a smelter, where furnaces

644
00:38:10,960 --> 00:38:14,060
refine it to 99.6% purity.

645
00:38:14,061 --> 00:38:19,300
And it's cast into 700-pound plates called anodes.

646
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:23,740
But 99.6% is not pure enough.

647
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:27,380
Racks of anodes are lowered into an acid solution interlaced

648
00:38:27,480 --> 00:38:29,740
with stainless steel cathodes.

649
00:38:29,840 --> 00:38:33,320
For 10 days, an electric current passes between anode

650
00:38:33,420 --> 00:38:37,720
and cathode, causing copper ions to dissolve off the anode

651
00:38:37,820 --> 00:38:44,060
and redeposit on the cathode as 99.99% pure.

652
00:38:44,061 --> 00:38:47,560
One ton of ore from the mine has yielded 12 pounds

653
00:38:47,561 --> 00:38:48,500
of refined copper.

654
00:38:52,740 --> 00:38:55,280
Copper is easily shaped, molded, and drawn

655
00:38:55,281 --> 00:38:58,640
into electrical wires.

656
00:38:58,641 --> 00:39:01,340
Residential wiring is covered in white, orange,

657
00:39:01,341 --> 00:39:06,140
or yellow insulation to identify its gauge.

658
00:39:06,141 --> 00:39:09,380
Commercial bundles contain up to 61 separate strands

659
00:39:09,381 --> 00:39:12,720
to deliver high voltage power.

660
00:39:12,721 --> 00:39:17,940
If we had one solid conductor, something of this diameter,

661
00:39:18,600 --> 00:39:22,640
it would be so difficult to bend, to pull around corners

662
00:39:22,641 --> 00:39:24,020
to get to where you need to go.

663
00:39:24,240 --> 00:39:25,840
It would be difficult to work with.

664
00:39:26,120 --> 00:39:29,440
When you have a stranded conductor of many individuals,

665
00:39:30,100 --> 00:39:33,340
it becomes flexible enough that you can pull it through conduits

666
00:39:33,341 --> 00:39:34,640
to get to where you need to go.

667
00:39:35,800 --> 00:39:37,880
If we live in a global village,

668
00:39:38,580 --> 00:39:40,340
copper is the glue that connects us.

669
00:39:40,341 --> 00:39:44,780
It allows communication around our world.

670
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:49,080
The heavy metal nickel is helping us travel to other worlds.

671
00:39:52,180 --> 00:39:54,420
Copper is the only metal besides gold

672
00:39:54,520 --> 00:39:56,760
that has a distinctive natural color.

673
00:39:56,860 --> 00:39:58,860
Other metals are either gray or white.

674
00:40:06,760 --> 00:40:10,300
The heavy metal nickel has an atomic number of 24.

675
00:40:10,340 --> 00:40:12,980
and its unique combination of light weight, corrosion

676
00:40:13,080 --> 00:40:14,820
resistance, and extreme strength

677
00:40:14,920 --> 00:40:17,920
have made it highly valued.

678
00:40:18,020 --> 00:40:19,720
Much of the Earth's nickel deposits

679
00:40:19,820 --> 00:40:22,480
date back to an ancient cataclysmic event that

680
00:40:22,580 --> 00:40:24,860
shook a region in what is now eastern Canada.

681
00:40:33,560 --> 00:40:37,100
A meteor struck the ground near Sudbury, Ontario, somewhere

682
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:40,340
around 2 billion years ago.

683
00:40:40,341 --> 00:40:43,840
And in the process of striking the ground,

684
00:40:43,940 --> 00:40:45,720
brought the nickel that it had in it,

685
00:40:45,820 --> 00:40:48,340
but also caused the nickel from under the ground

686
00:40:48,440 --> 00:40:51,880
to come up through flows from the Earth's interior.

687
00:40:51,980 --> 00:40:56,860
30% of the world's nickel comes from the giant impact crater

688
00:40:56,960 --> 00:40:58,260
in Sudbury.

689
00:40:58,360 --> 00:41:00,420
And much of that nickel is devoted

690
00:41:00,520 --> 00:41:02,420
to high-performance aviation.

691
00:41:02,421 --> 00:41:03,320
aviation.

692
00:41:08,720 --> 00:41:12,860
Nickel is the indispensable element in jet turbine blades,

693
00:41:12,960 --> 00:41:16,360
devices that must rotate at 10,000 revolutions per minute

694
00:41:16,460 --> 00:41:20,300
for up to 16 hours without deforming in the slightest,

695
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:24,480
and whose failure could cause death and destruction.

696
00:41:24,580 --> 00:41:27,580
Here you have a blade spinning around at enormous speed.

697
00:41:27,680 --> 00:41:29,940
And if you've ever spun on a merry-go-round,

698
00:41:29,941 --> 00:41:32,080
you know how hard it is to hold on.

699
00:41:32,180 --> 00:41:34,580
The outside of a turbine blade has to hold on

700
00:41:34,680 --> 00:41:37,380
to the inside of a turbine blade at 1,000 degrees.

701
00:41:37,480 --> 00:41:41,680
And it has to do this in the presence of a streaming flow

702
00:41:41,780 --> 00:41:43,180
of hot reactive gases.

703
00:41:47,800 --> 00:41:51,060
Nickel super alloys used in turbine blades like these

704
00:41:51,160 --> 00:41:53,560
are actually stronger at 1,200 degrees

705
00:41:53,660 --> 00:41:54,560
than at room temperature.

706
00:41:54,561 --> 00:41:58,060
temperature.

707
00:41:58,160 --> 00:42:01,900
The process of making turbines, known as investment casting,

708
00:42:02,000 --> 00:42:04,900
involves a mix of ancient and futuristic technologies.

709
00:42:08,300 --> 00:42:10,500
The investment casting process is very old.

710
00:42:10,600 --> 00:42:12,620
It was essentially developed by the Egyptians.

711
00:42:12,720 --> 00:42:15,220
They used it to make a lot of their statues.

712
00:42:15,320 --> 00:42:19,360
They'd take beeswax, form the statues out of the beeswax,

713
00:42:19,460 --> 00:42:22,760
and then probably went down to the Nile River, got some clay,

714
00:42:22,761 --> 00:42:24,600
and shape the clay around it.

715
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:27,200
Then they'd melt gold or silver or bronze

716
00:42:27,201 --> 00:42:29,640
and pour it into these clay molds.

717
00:42:33,440 --> 00:42:36,540
Today's engine castings are made using the same process,

718
00:42:36,940 --> 00:42:38,740
but with much greater precision.

719
00:42:40,740 --> 00:42:43,180
Molds for turbine blades are computer designed,

720
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:45,880
with prototypes created by a laser

721
00:42:45,881 --> 00:42:48,820
that cures photosensitive resin in layers

722
00:42:48,821 --> 00:42:50,660
four thousandths of an inch,

723
00:42:50,661 --> 00:42:53,160
1 third the width of a human hair.

724
00:42:56,160 --> 00:42:58,660
The efficiency of a jet engine is directly related

725
00:42:58,760 --> 00:43:00,700
to its operating temperature.

726
00:43:00,800 --> 00:43:03,000
The more heat turbine blades can withstand,

727
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:05,500
the farther and faster a plane can fly.

728
00:43:08,320 --> 00:43:11,840
Military jet engines can operate at well above 2,000 degrees

729
00:43:11,940 --> 00:43:14,320
Fahrenheit, and there are constant efforts

730
00:43:14,420 --> 00:43:16,220
to raise the ceiling even higher.

731
00:43:16,221 --> 00:43:19,220
higher.

732
00:43:19,320 --> 00:43:22,260
Heavy metals have always helped us advance our goals

733
00:43:22,360 --> 00:43:24,900
and extend our reach.

734
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:27,840
They've been on the cutting edge all throughout the 20th

735
00:43:27,940 --> 00:43:30,360
century, and certainly will be through the 21st century as

736
00:43:30,460 --> 00:43:31,240
well.

737
00:43:31,340 --> 00:43:34,440
And whether that's in high-tech metals for aircraft

738
00:43:34,540 --> 00:43:37,780
applications or spacecraft, they are involved in almost

739
00:43:37,880 --> 00:43:40,440
everything that we do and everything that surrounds us.

740
00:43:40,441 --> 00:43:46,280
Our lone kinship with heavy metals

741
00:43:46,380 --> 00:43:51,320
made possible the giant leap of July 20, 1969.

742
00:43:54,760 --> 00:43:57,820
When Apollo 11 landed on the moon,

743
00:43:57,920 --> 00:44:00,420
the lunar module was coated with a nickel steel

744
00:44:00,520 --> 00:44:04,300
heat-resistant alloy to protect it from the sun's rays.

745
00:44:07,040 --> 00:44:09,640
The heavy metals that originated in deep space

746
00:44:09,641 --> 00:44:14,680
space have been transformed by mankind into tools that could

747
00:44:14,740 --> 00:44:16,580
reach back into the heavens.

